	That install applies to "Powermonger" © 1990 Bullfrog.

	That install requires WHDLoad and DIC to be in the path. Both
	can be found in the whdload package.

	You need 0.5 MB ChipMem and 0.5 MB OtherMem.
	You can quit the game by pressing ESC or F10 (key can be changed with
	Tooltype "QuitKey=")

	Manual protection has been removed. One Access Fault fixed. Savegame
	redirected to harddisk. Keyboard routine fixed.

	To extract your savegames from a floppy :
		create a disk image using DIC from your savegame disk
		enter the directory where "Disk.1" is stored, and
		execute "sgsaver", all valid savegames are extracted,
		move them now to the Powermonger save directory ...

	Thanx to Peter Schreck and Harley Kingston for media support.

	If you have problems with this installation please report them via
	the Bug-Report-Form which can be accessed via
		http://www.whdload.de/

	for full WHDLoad package and other installs refer to:
		http://www.whdload.de/
	or	aminet:dev/misc/whdload#?
	and	aminet:game/patch/

	version 1.5 (07.11.2026): game bug fixes
	- Fixes for faults in the game's own code, applied by default. Each is
	  a few bytes changed where the original reads the wrong register,
	  width or bound; none changes the game's balance or data:
	  - a supply run returned to a spot four cells south of the town
	    clicked (the stored cell gained 256 from a dirty register)
	  - a captain of a slow tribe walked away from his target in rain or
	    snow at 6 units a tick (speed 10-16 = -6, stored unsigned and
	    then taken as a signed step); he now walks toward it at speed 2
	  - a man turned back by water wandered off in a direction unrelated
	    to his facing (the step was looked up by the whole random draw
	    instead of the facing byte)
	  - a shooter's facing was set to the low byte of his target's y
	    coordinate instead of the aim angle
	  - a planted spy stayed on his home town's resident list, which then
	    led into the host town's residents; he is now unlinked on planting
	    and relinked on unmasking
	  - the first sheep of every land had the handle that also ends a
	    flock chain, so no shepherd could ever gather it; animal record 0
	    is now left unused (39 animals per land instead of 40)
	  - a sheep blocked by the shore then drifted one unit a tick faster
	    than its pace
	  - a loose sheep in the southern quarter of the map never moved (the
	    row bound was 96 on a map 128 rows high)
	  - a short, near-diagonal shot flew the wrong way when its step
	    exceeded 127; the step is clamped
	  - the computer's reinforcement army was ordered to a mirrored cell
	    (the cell was packed y then x)
	  - the settlement ring scan could replace a settlement's owner by
	    half its headcount for the rest of the scan
	- Four optional corrections that change how the game plays, each off
	  by default:
	  - diplomacy: a clash between tribes set their dispositions to the
	    friendliest value (a signed -8 read as +248, and the entry before
	    the right one), so an alliance after a war was free; and the
	    computer's choice of target read a meaningless byte instead of the
	    disposition. Fixed: clashes breed hostility and the computer reads
	    real dispositions
	  - alliance gifts: goods offered with an alliance were destroyed;
	    they now reach the receiving town
	  - target choice: a fighter kept a record number as his "best
	    distance", so a nearer target replaced the old one only by
	    accident; he now picks the nearest
	  - AI town scoring: the computer scored its own towns with a dirty
	    32-bit dividend and sent captains to distant towns for men and
	    food; the division is now clean
	- The switches are in WHDLoad's splash window ("Original game bugs:
	  Fixed/Kept" and the four "Also fix" boxes), saved to the icon when
	  started from Workbench, or as tooltypes: CUSTOM4=1 keeps the
	  original bugs; CUSTOM5 is a bit mask of the four options (1
	  diplomacy, 2 alliance gifts, 4 target choice, 8 AI town scoring).
	- Every correction was checked against the unpatched game under
	  emulation, before and after the change.

	version 1.4.1 (19.02.2025):
	- made Manual.pdf compatible to APDF, thanks Retroplay

	version 1.4 (10.02.2025) done by Paraj and Wepl:
	- Use fast RAM to speed up rendering (configurable, defaults to
	  on if 030+). If the game is now too fast, "CUSTOM2" can be used
	  to limit the update rate, e.g. CUSTOM2=4 for 12.5Hz if PAL.
	- Added trainer options to start with all maps and win on retire.
	- Copy protection dialog is automatically dismissed.
	- More icons added
	- Various docs/maps added
	- New install script
	- Source available: https://github.com/wepl/winstalls

	version 1.3 (17.7.2002) done by Wepl:
	- sound replay in intro fixed for 68040/60

